.TH mdu 1 "15 October 2026" "Version 1.0"

.SH NAME
mdu \- my disk usage, show storage device space used by a file tree

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mdu
.RI "[-j " number_of_threads "] " file " ..."

.SH DESCRIPTION
Show the number of 512-byte blocks allocated to each file specified in the
command line. If a directory is specified the total block count of the whole
directory sub-tree is shown. Symbolic links are counted but not followed,
meaning that the file a symbolic link points to is not counted unless it exists
in the same file tree. Hard links are counted as many times as they appear in
the file tree.
.br
This is equivalent to the du(1) command:
.EX
	du -s -l -B512 [file ...]
.EE
.P
The option -j may be used to specify the number of threads to use for
calculating the size of each file tree. If -j is not given the number of threads
used is 1.

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI "-j " number_of_threads
Number of threads used for calculating a file tree

.SH SOLUTION
The solution is based around a job stack shared between all worker threads.
A job here is defined as a file path to query. Threads pop jobs from the stack
one at a time and query the file block count with lstat(2). The block count is
added to a thread sum. If the file is a directory then the path to each file in
the directory is pushed as a new job onto the stack. Once there are no more jobs
to do the sum of the thread block count sums is printed.
.P
Push and pop operations on the job stack are guarded by a mutex, ensuring only
one thread at a time can modify the stack. This is an obvious performance
bottleneck in the solution.
The reason for using a job stack rather than a job queue is that a stack is
simpler to implement.
.P
A condition variable (see pthread_cond_init(3)) predicated on a counter of
active threads is used to ensure the full pool of worker threads are available
for jobs as needed. Any thread that finds the job stack empty marks itself as
inactive by decrementing the active threads counter and waits for the condition
variable. When a thread pushes a job on the stack it sends a signal to one
waiting thread (pthread_cond_signal(3)). A thread that wakes up from waiting on
the condition variable marks itself active by increasing the active threads
counter and continues to popping the job stack, unless counter has reached zero.
The last thread that marks itself as inactive broadcasts as signal to all
waiting threads (phtread_cond_broadcast(3)) and exits. Threads waking up from
waiting on the condition variable to find that there are no active threads,
exit. Operations on the condition variable and the predicate are guarded by an
associated mutex to avoid race conditions, as per the pthread_cond_wait(3)
documentation.

.SS Main algorithm
.IP 1. 3
Push file path on job stack
.IP 2.
Initialize counter of active threads to number of threads
.IP 3.
Create threads
.IP 4.
In each thread:
.RS
.IP 4.1. 5
While job stack has jobs:
.RS
.IP 4.1.1. 7
Pop file path from job stack
.IP 4.1.2.
lstat(2) file path
.IP 4.1.3.
Add block count to thread sum
.IP 4.1.4.
If file is directory:
.RS
.IP 4.1.4.1. 9
Open directory stream with opendir(3)
.IP 4.1.4.2.
While directory stream has entries:
.br
(see readdir(3))
.RS
.IP 4.1.4.2.1. 11
Read filename from directory stream entry
.IP 4.1.4.2.2.
Concatenate directory path with file name, with '/' in between
.IP 4.1.4.2.3.
Push file path onto job stack
.IP 4.1.4.2.3.
Signal one waiting thread condition (pthread_cond_signal(3))
.RE
.IP 4.1.4.3
Close directory stream with closedir(3)
.RE
.RE
.IP 4.2.
Decrease counter of active threads by 1
.IP 4.3.
If no active threads:
.RS
.IP 4.3.1 7
Broadcast condition signal to all waiting threads (pthread_cond_broadcast(3))
.IP 4.3.2
Go to step 4.6.
.RE
.IP 4.4
Wait for condition signal (pthread_cond_wait(3))
.IP 4.5.
If any thread is active:
.RS
.IP 4.5.1 7
Increase counter of active threads by 1
.IP 4.5.2
Go to step 4.1.
.RE
.IP 4.6.
Exit thread with block count sum as thread result
.RE
.IP 5.
Join threads
.IP 6.
Sum thread results

